## JIT Compile Time Gap Investigation (2026-07-08)

SDS sub_jit=400.7ms vs NB sub_jit=207.9ms (+193ms). Final JIT nearly equal
(+16ms). The gap is entirely from subquery JIT, not final query JIT.

Root cause: DuckDB Prepare() cost, not template cache misses.

Cache key dump analysis (AQP_DUMP_CACHE_KEYS, release build):
| Metric | SDS | NB |
|---|---|---|
| Total compilations/pass | 34.7 | 41.5 |
| Cache hits/pass | 12.9 (37.3%) | 9.3 (22.5%) |
| Cache misses/pass | 21.7 | 32.2 |
| Unique plan shapes | 326 | 483 |
| Mean steps/plan (miss) | 2.81 | 2.45 |
| Mean HTs/plan (miss) | 1.80 | 1.40 |
| Mean plan size (miss) | 479 chars | 369 chars |
| Per-sub JIT cost | ~1.0 ms | ~0.5 ms |
| Total subqueries (113 queries) | 524 | 625 |

SDS has FEWER compilations, HIGHER hit rate, FEWER misses — but each
compilation takes ~2× longer. Growth heuristic creates larger subqueries
(more tables), making Prepare() ~3× slower per subquery.

Why not fixable: (1) disabling growth → +0.4s exe regression (v4);
(2) spec-jit gated to NODE_BASED; (3) template canonicalization only
~40-50ms gain. Spec-jit (Phase 2b Step 2) is the path forward.

