## Phase 2b Step 2: Spec-jit for SDS — INVESTIGATED, NOT VIABLE (2026-07-11)

Goal: speculatively compile the next subquery during current Execute,
overlapping bg Prepare+TPDE compile with Execute(i) to close the +193 ms
JIT gap vs NB. Currently gated to NODE_BASED at ir_query_splitter.cpp
:1043, :1635, :1838, :2664, :2737.

### Proposed mechanism (pre-investigation)

1. Prediction: after launching Execute(i), run PlanNext on a deep-cloned
   join graph with estimated temp cardinality → BuildSubIRForCluster →
   GenerateSQL → launch bg Prepare+TPDE compile.
2. Validation: after Execute(i) returns actual cardinality, re-run PlanNext
   with actual state → compare SQL. Match → HIT; mismatch → MISS.
3. Consumption: same as NB (CheckSpeculativeResult, SetQjitSpecHit,
   compensate_fast TPDE fallback on card_miss).

### Investigation results (2026-07-11)

Ran 17 representative JOB queries (17a, 19a, 25c, 26c, 7c, 16b, 9d, 18c,
33a, 29a, 14a, 10c, 20a, 22d, 30a, 31a, 32b) through SDS debug build,
capturing all PlanNext group decisions and UpdateRemainingIR actual
cardinalities. 68 iterations total, 51 iter→iter transitions analyzed.

Finding 1 — estimation accuracy is poor:
  Only 21% of iterations have est within 2× of actual (card guard threshold).
  49% within 5×. Many errors 100-1000× (keyword×movie_keyword: 1241×,
  complete_cast chains: 1510×, 29a iter 8: 87,480×).

Finding 2 — 75% of transitions are temp-dependent:
  Of 51 transitions, 38 (75%) have the next group including the current
  iteration's temp. Only 13 (25%) pick an independent base-table pair.
  For dependent transitions, the estimated temp cardinality directly affects
  which group PlanNext picks (the DP tree shape changes).

Finding 3 — the timing gap is fatal (the kill shot):
  NB spec-jit works because PeekNextSubquery reads from a pre-built subquery
  queue at PostPrepareHook time (after Prepare(i), before Execute(i)). The
  bg compile overlaps the ENTIRE Execute(i) window (10-100ms).

  SDS cannot replicate this because:
  - PlanNext walks the remaining IR tree via CollectRelations/CollectEdges
    → requires the actual updated IR with temp scan nodes
  - UpdateRemainingIR runs AFTER Execute(i) → the earliest SDS can know
    the next subquery is after Execute(i) finishes
  - Phase B (post-UpdateRemainingIR) bg compile has only ~0.1ms head start
    before the next iteration's inline Prepare starts
  - Net savings per hit: ~0.1ms (not ~1.0ms as the pre-investigation
    theoretical model assumed)
  - Over 524 subqueries at 100% hit: ~52ms. Realistic (21% card-guard
    pass, high SQL mismatch): 0-10ms, minus miss overhead → net negative

  BuildSubIRForCluster cannot run on the pre-update IR for dependent
  transitions (75%) because the temp scan node doesn't exist yet.

Finding 4 — NB spec-jit already regresses in this regime:
  NB spec-jit measured: JIT +206ms, EXE +155ms = +373ms total regression
  (tmpl-cache regime). Root cause: card_misses frequent, compensate_fast
  adds inline TPDE compile, bg thread CPU contention. SDS would have a
  strictly worse starting position (smaller overlap window + worse
  prediction accuracy).

### Conclusion

NOT VIABLE. The structural difference between NB and SDS — NB has a
pre-built subquery queue enabling prediction during Execute, while SDS's
feedback-adaptive DP requires actual temp cardinality — means the overlap
window that makes spec-jit valuable does not exist for SDS. The +193ms
JIT gap is structural (engine Prepare cost scaling with subquery size),
not recoverable without engine-side changes (e.g., incremental Prepare
reuse).

## Phase 2b Step 3: First-Subquery Bg-Compile for SDS (2026-07-11, DONE)

Spec-jit for the general case is not viable (Step 2), but iteration 1 is
the exception: the FIRST split decision per query depends only on
preprocessed base stats (no temp feedback yet) → 100% deterministic at
cross-query-prep time. The bg thread can run SplitIR for iteration 1 and
bg-compile the first sub-SQL exactly like NB's PrepareNextQuery.

### Mechanism

- `PrepareNextQueryTopDown` (ir_query_splitter.cpp): after bg Preprocess,
  before MovePreprocessState (PlanNext reads table_card_ etc.), run
  `bg_splitter.SplitIR()` → ConvertIRToSQL → `BgCompileFirstSubquery`.
  Extraction + split_iteration/executed_tables handed over via
  `CrossQueryPrepResult` (first_extraction, td_split_iteration,
  td_executed_tables); consumed in ExecuteSplitLoop as
  `precomputed_extraction_` (skips ReorderBeforeSplit + SplitIR for iter 1).
- `BgCompileFirstSubquery`: new shared static helper factored out of NB's
  PrepareNextQuery inline block. Path A: query-jit via
  SpeculativeQueryJitCompile; Path B: other jit levels via Prepare +
  RegisterJITImpl; Path C: interpreter plain Prepare. NB now calls the
  same helper (behavior unchanged, 113/113 verified).
- Iteration-1 HIT consumption reuses the existing strategy-agnostic path
  (~ir_query_splitter.cpp:2580): SetQjitSpecHit + ExecuteSQLandCreateTempTable
  (Path A) or ExecuteSpeculativeAndCreateTempTable (Path B/C). Writes
  "0.000, " to generate_sub_sql column — CSV shape unchanged.
- Bg-thread engine safety: `TopDownSplitter::SetBgMode(true)` makes both
  `BatchGetEstimatedCosts` EXPLAIN paths in PlanNext throw; the caller
  catches and aborts the pre-split entirely, so the foreground re-decides
  with identical inputs (col_distinct_hints_ mutations are idempotent
  max-accumulations → no state divergence from an aborted attempt).
- Kill switch: env `AQP_TD_NO_PRESPLIT` disables the pre-split (used for
  same-session A/B).

### Bug fixed en route

`ExecuteSpeculativeAndCreateTempTable` (duckdb_adapter.cpp:1875) deref'd
`planner->binder` unguarded. NB always injects a planner via SetPlanner
(ir_query_splitter.cpp:897, NB-only); SDS never does and ResetQueryState
nulls it → segfault in the topdown expr-jit run (died after 10a). Fix:
null-planner guard with the same monotonic table-index fallback as
ExecuteSQLandCreateTempTable's replay path.

### Results (same-session A/B via AQP_TD_NO_PRESPLIT, canonical parser)

| config | MW | jit | exe | total |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| OFF (v12+cqp rerun) | 0.06 | 0.517 | 4.701 | 5.276 |
| ON (v14) | 0.06 | 0.435 | 4.740 | 5.236 |
| delta | ≈0 | **-82ms** | +39ms | **-40ms** |

- jit -82ms: first subquery per query compiled in bg (overlaps prev
  query's Execute).
- exe +39ms: bg TPDE codegen CPU contention during foreground execution
  (12-core box, DuckDB saturates cores). Net win -40ms → KEEP.
- The first ON measurement's apparent +200ms exe was cross-session drift
  (same-session OFF exe 4.701 vs prior session 4.609; ±60-90ms variance).
- SDS jit gap to NB narrows from +193ms to ~+100ms.
- Correctness: 113/113 for topdown query-jit tpde tmpl-cache, topdown
  expr-jit tpde tmpl-cache, and node-based query-jit tpde tmpl-cache
  (regression check for the Path A/B/C refactor).
- CSVs: v14 = ..._breakdown_time_log_v14.csv; OFF baseline =
  ..._v12cqp_rerun.csv.

Synergy note: with jit now cheaper, the dropped Opt#1 (>=4 → >=3 split
gate; exe -43 / jit +37 / MW +23) tradeoff may flip — revisit in the exe
outlier investigation.

## Phase 2b Step 4: Kill all SDS qjit fallbacks (2026-07-11, DONE, v15)

Outlier decomposition (task #5) found SDS had 4 query-jit compilation
fallbacks over the suite (NB: 0/603), all mapping to top loss queries:
- 10c final: `fallback:join:condition-sides` (ran interpreted, ~136ms)
- 26c final + 26a/26b temp5: `fallback:expr:Unknown#8`

Root causes and fixes (middleware compiler only, no splitter/DuckDB
changes; NB shares the code but never produced these shapes):

1. **VarComparisonNode filters** (expr:Unknown#8 = SimplestNodeType 8).
   SDS-specific: folding two joined tables into one temp turns their join
   predicate into a same-row col=col FILTER qual (e.g. 26c temp4
   `movie_keyword_9_movie_id = temp3_..._movie_info_idx_8_movie_id`).
   The Analyzer whitelist accepted it but per-step CheckFilterStrict did
   not. Fix:
   - query_jit_steps.cpp CheckFilterStrict: accept VarComparisonNode when
     both attrs are INT32 (ExpectedDtype) and op ∈ {=,<>,<,>,<=,>=};
     registers both attrs as source cols.
   - ir_to_llvm.cpp: new EmitVarVar (integer family loads sign-extended
     to i64, ICmp) + EmitExpr dispatch case with nested
     EmitNullGuardedLeaf on BOTH columns (NULL on either side → false;
     qjit has no downstream re-filter).
   - Cache keys need no change: SerializeExprTemplate falls through to
     Print(false) (no constants → nothing to parameterize) and
     BuildParamsFromExpr correctly allocates no params.

2. **Projection-binding blindness** (join:condition-sides). 10c's final
   plan has a DuckDB compressed-materialization PROJECTION
   (`__internal_compress_integral_utinyint`) between joins; the join
   condition above it references the projection's binder index
   (l=(3,0)char_name.id r=(11,1)person_role_id — 11 = the projection),
   which CollectTables (scans/chunks only) can't classify → fallback.
   The converter already erases compress/decompress wrappers
   (UnwrapToColumnRef), so projection target_list[k] IS the underlying
   attr. Fix in query_jit_steps.cpp PlanBuilder:
   - CollectProjRemap pre-pass: (proj table_index, position) → underlying
     attr, over the whole statement tree.
   - ThroughProj chain-resolve applied at join-condition classification
     and at ResolveAttr entry (covers ht keys, probe keys, outputs, agg
     args). RegisterFilterAttr intentionally NOT remapped: filter exprs
     are emitted from raw IR pointers, codegen could not follow the remap
     — the existing source-table check keeps those falling back safely.
   - Also enriched the condition-sides Fail reason with attr coordinates
     (debug-only visibility).

